          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#the-cycle" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. The Annual Cycle</a>
          <a href="#fake-discounts" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. Spotting a Discount That Is Not One</a>
          <a href="#what-discounts" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. What Actually Discounts, and What Does Not</a>
          <a href="#buying-well" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Buying Well in a Sale</a>
          <a href="#black-friday-vs-january" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Black Friday or January?</a>
          <a href="#beyond-the-sale" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Levers That Beat the Sale</a>
          <a href="#checklist" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. The Sale-Day Checklist</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A treadmill is one of the few purchases where waiting is reliably worth money, because
          demand for it is intensely seasonal and every retailer knows exactly when that season
          starts. The useful question is not whether to wait but which of the year's several sale
          events is actually the low.
        </p>

        <div id="the-cycle" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Annual Cycle</h2>
<p>
            Treadmill demand is not evenly spread and the retail calendar is built around that.
            Four moments matter.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Black Friday through Cyber Monday.</strong> The most visible, the most
            advertised, and genuinely good for some machines. It is also the point at which demand
            is about to rise rather than fall, which is why treadmill discounting in late November
            is often shallower than the headline percentages on electronics suggest.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Late December into January.</strong> The demand peak. Resolutions, gift money
            and gym-avoidance all land at once, floor space expands, and selection is at its widest
            of the year. Prices are competitive because everyone is competing, but this is the one
            time you are shopping alongside everybody else and stock on popular models moves.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Model-year changeover.</strong> The genuinely underrated one. Manufacturers
            refresh lines on their own schedules, and when a new version is announced the outgoing
            model is cleared — often at the deepest discount that machine will ever see. As our
            <a href="/horizon-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">brand guide</a> notes,
            annual refreshes in this category are overwhelmingly console and software changes while
            the frame, motor, deck and rollers carry over unchanged. Buying the outgoing model is
            frequently the single best value decision available.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Late spring into summer.</strong> Demand collapses, people go outside, and
            retailers are sitting on machines that take up a great deal of floor space. Selection
            narrows sharply but the remaining stock is cheap, and the secondhand market floods with
            January purchases that did not stick — our
            <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">timing
            guide</a> covers that pattern in detail, and our
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used analysis</a>
            explains why it matters more than any sale event.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fake-discounts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Spotting a Discount That Is Not One</h2>
<p>
            The percentage on a label is arithmetic against a reference price the seller selected,
            and there are several ways that reference can be flattering.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The reset list price.</strong> A machine sells at one price for months, the list
            price is raised shortly before a sale event, and the discount is then calculated against
            the new figure. The sale price may be identical to what it was in October.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The manufacturer's suggested price nobody charges.</strong> A perfectly legal
            and extremely common reference point that has not been the real price at any retailer
            for years. A large percentage off an irrelevant number is still an irrelevant number.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The bundle.</strong> A discount that arrives with a mat, a heart rate strap and
            three months of a subscription attached. Price the machine alone and compare that, since
            the bundled items are usually valued at list and are rarely things you would have
            bought.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The retailer-specific model.</strong> Covered at length in our
            <a href="/costco-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warehouse guide</a> and
            <a href="/dicks-sporting-goods-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Dick's
            guide</a>: large retailers carry variants with their own model numbers, and a
            differently-specified machine cannot be compared against the reviews of the mainstream
            one. This is the most consequential trap on this page because it is invisible in a price
            comparison.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The test that cuts through all of it:</strong> check the price against its own
            history, not against the label. Browser extensions and price-tracking sites keep
            histories for most major retailers, and thirty seconds tells you whether today's number
            has been seen before. If a machine has sat at this price twice already this year, the
            sale is a label.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-discounts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Actually Discounts, and What Does Not</h2>
<p>
            Not every treadmill moves in a sale, and knowing which do saves a lot of waiting.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Mid-market machines discount hardest.</strong> The crowded, competitive tier
            where several brands offer broadly similar hardware is where retailers have both the
            margin and the motive to cut. If you are shopping in our
            <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-$1,000</a>
            or <a href="/treadmills-under-1500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-$1,500</a>
            bands, waiting is well rewarded.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Outgoing model years discount hardest of all</strong>, for the reasons above.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Subscription-led machines discount the hardware and not the subscription.</strong>
            This is important. A screen-led treadmill can be cut sharply because the recurring
            revenue is where the business is — and the five-year cost barely moves, since the
            membership is the larger number. Our
            <a href="/peloton-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Peloton guide</a> runs
            that arithmetic. A deep hardware discount on a subscription machine is a smaller saving
            than it appears.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Premium and commercial machines discount least.</strong> Lower volumes, dealer
            distribution, less promotional pricing. Our
            <a href="/commercial-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial guide</a>
            and <a href="/precor-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Precor guide</a>
            cover a market where the real value route is secondhand rather than seasonal.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walking pads discount often and shallowly.</strong> They are cheap and they
            churn, so there is frequently something on offer — but the absolute saving is small, and
            at this end of the market the specification checks in our
            <a href="/walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">category guide</a> matter far
            more than the price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-well" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Buying Well in a Sale</h2>
<p>
            The discipline that separates a good purchase from a regretted one is all done before
            the sale starts.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Decide the specification first, then the price.</strong> This is the whole
            thing. Work out the deck length your stride needs, the motor rating, the capacity and
            whether you want incline — our
            <a href="/treadmill-buyers-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buyer's guide</a>
            is the checklist — and shortlist two or three machines that meet it. Then watch those.
            A sale is a good time to buy a machine you already chose and a terrible time to choose
            one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Start tracking prices weeks in advance.</strong> You cannot recognise a genuine
            low without a baseline, and the baseline takes time to establish.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Know your walk-away number.</strong> Written down, before the event.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the model number against the manufacturer.</strong> Full designation
            including any suffix, compared on deck dimensions, continuous motor rating, capacity and
            warranty. Three minutes, and it is the check that stops you buying a different machine
            from the one you researched.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Add delivery and assembly to the comparison.</strong> A cheaper machine with
            kerbside delivery can cost more than a dearer one delivered to the room and assembled —
            and a boxed treadmill has no good handholds. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">dimensions
            guide</a> covers measuring the route before the van arrives.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the return terms for a large item</strong>, which are not always the same
            as general merchandise, and are the thing you will care about most if the deck turns out
            too short.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="black-friday-vs-january" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Black Friday or January?</h2>
<p>
            The direct comparison, since these are the two events most people weigh.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Black Friday has the deeper advertised discounts and the narrower treadmill
            selection.</strong> Fitness equipment competes for promotional attention with
            electronics, which is where the real Black Friday pricing energy goes. Retailers know
            the treadmill season is about to begin, so there is less urgency to clear stock.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>January has the widest selection and the most competition.</strong> Every
            retailer is chasing the same buyers at the same moment, floor space expands, and
            manufacturers run their own promotions. Prices are genuinely competitive, but you are
            shopping alongside everyone else and popular models go out of stock.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>For most buyers, the honest advice is: buy in whichever of the two you are
            actually ready for.</strong> The difference between them is usually smaller than the
            difference between buying a well-chosen machine and a poorly-chosen one, and a
            treadmill bought in a rush in November because the label said fifty percent is not a
            bargain.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>And if you can wait past both, do.</strong> Model-year changeover and late
            spring are where the genuinely large discounts live, and late spring additionally opens
            the secondhand market described in our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used
            buying checklist</a> — where the same money buys substantially more hardware than any
            sale event delivers.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The one case for buying in November regardless:</strong> if you will genuinely
            use the machine through the winter, two months of use is worth more than a marginal
            saving. A treadmill bought in May and used in September has lost you the season it was
            for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="beyond-the-sale" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Levers That Beat the Sale</h2>
<p>
            Several things move the price more than the date does, and they are available all year.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Price matching.</strong> Large retailers commonly match named competitors,
            sometimes within a window after purchase. Our
            <a href="/dicks-sporting-goods-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">retail
            guide</a> covers asking properly, and it can deliver sale pricing outside a sale.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Open-box and floor models.</strong> Frequently a larger discount than any
            seasonal event, on a machine that has been assembled once. Inspect it as you would a
            used machine and confirm the warranty position.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Refurbished from the manufacturer.</strong> Wear parts replaced, a limited
            warranty, delivery handled — most of the used-market value with recourse attached.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The used market, which beats every sale.</strong> Our
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new versus used
            analysis</a> shows secondhand buys roughly twice the hardware per dollar, and the supply
            peaks a few months after January for entirely predictable reasons.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Skipping the subscription.</strong> On a screen-led machine, five years of
            membership dwarfs any hardware discount. Choosing a
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">machine
            that does not need one</a> saves more than the best Black Friday deal you will find.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>And buying the outgoing model year</strong>, which is the cheapest way to get
            current hardware and is available whenever a refresh happens rather than on a fixed
            date.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="checklist" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. The Sale-Day Checklist</h2>
<p>
            <strong>One. Shortlist decided in advance</strong>, on specification, from our
            <a href="/treadmill-buyers-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buyer's
            checklist</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Two. Price history checked</strong> for each shortlisted machine, so you know
            whether today's number is genuinely a low.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Three. Model number verified</strong> against the manufacturer's own
            specification — deck, continuous motor rating, capacity, warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Four. Delivery and assembly priced in</strong>, and the route measured.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Five. Five-year cost calculated</strong> if there is a subscription involved,
            not the sticker.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Six. Return terms confirmed</strong> for a large item.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Seven. Walk-away number honoured.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            And once it arrives, the thing that decides what the purchase is actually worth is not
            what you paid — it is whether you
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubricate it
            on schedule</a>. A machine bought at full price and maintained outlasts a bargain that
            ran dry, by years.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Black Friday the best time to buy a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not reliably. It has the deepest advertised discounts and a narrower fitness selection, because treadmill demand is about to rise rather than fall and retailers know it. The genuinely large discounts tend to come at model-year changeover, when outgoing stock is cleared, and in late spring when demand collapses and floor space is needed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I tell if a treadmill discount is real?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Check the price against its own history rather than against the label. Price-tracking sites and browser extensions keep histories for most major retailers, and thirty seconds tells you whether today's number has been seen before. A percentage is arithmetic against a reference the seller chose, and that reference is often a list price nobody has charged for years.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which treadmills discount the most?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mid-market machines in the crowded, competitive tiers, and outgoing model years above all — annual refreshes are overwhelmingly console changes while the frame, motor and deck carry over. Premium and commercial machines discount least. Subscription-led machines discount the hardware but not the membership, so the five-year cost barely moves.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy in November or wait until January?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                January has the widest selection and the most competition; November has the deeper advertised percentages and less urgency behind them. The difference between the two is usually smaller than the difference between a well-chosen machine and a rushed one. If you can wait past both, model-year changeover and late spring are where the real discounts are.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the retailer-specific model trap?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Large retailers frequently carry variants with their own model numbers that can differ in console, deck, motor rating or — most consequentially — warranty. A price comparison cannot see it. Write down the full model designation including any suffix, find it on the manufacturer's own site, and compare four specifications before assuming the reviews you read apply.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What saves more money than a sale?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Buying the outgoing model year, buying open-box or manufacturer-refurbished, using price matching outside a sale, buying secondhand — which gets roughly twice the hardware per dollar — and choosing a machine with no subscription. On a screen-led treadmill, five years of membership dwarfs any Black Friday hardware discount you will find.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying in a Sale</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">A sale is a good time to buy a machine you already chose and a bad time to choose one, and most of these follow from getting that the wrong way round.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing the machine during the sale</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Time pressure and a large percentage are a poor basis for a decision about deck length. Decide the specification weeks in advance — stride, motor rating, capacity, incline — shortlist two or three machines that meet it, and then watch only those. A treadmill bought in a rush because the label said half price is not a bargain if the deck is too short.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing against the label instead of the price history</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The percentage is arithmetic against a reference the retailer selected, which may be a list price nobody has charged for years or one raised shortly before the event. Check the machine against its own price history using a tracking site. If it has sat at this number twice already this year, the sale is a label rather than a discount.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking a deep discount on a subscription machine at face value</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Screen-led treadmills can be cut sharply because the recurring revenue is where the business is. Five years of membership is usually the larger number, so a big hardware discount moves the real total far less than it appears. Add sixty months of subscription to the sale price before comparing it against a machine that needs none.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting delivery, assembly and the route</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A cheaper machine delivered to the kerb can cost more in practice than a dearer one delivered to the room and assembled, and a boxed treadmill has no good handholds. Price the delivery tier into the comparison, and measure doorways, stairwell turns and ceiling height before the van arrives rather than after it has gone.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

